> The UX damage is real: a user saying "legal for legal stuff, comms for tone" gets blocked until they answer set-theoretic edge case questions about reviewer domains. Meanwhile, §7 applicability claims (artifact-scoped vs claim-scoped) are more defensible since they actually prevent no-op compiles. But §10's nine checks include several that are unfalsifiable in practice or self-referential—like cross-reference checks that audit the compiler's own prose rather than the artifact itself. And §16.3 mandates a drift notice in every compile header, taking up about six lines.
> 
> The real problem is that the ledger ships every turn with boilerplate that trains users to ignore it. Each diagnostic turn balloons to twenty lines of apparatus around just one to three questions, and the mandatory verbatim warnings about undetectable failures become ceremony that obscures the checks that actually matter.
> 
> Then there's the contradiction in §3.6: the system knows when it's done but is forbidden from saying so, while still asking the same questions again. That's interview creep masquerading as transparency. And the non-editorializing rule prevents recommending compilation while the compiler can't initiate exit either — a UX failure born from overcorrecting against pushiness.
> 
> Some questions shouldn't be asked at all: anything a gate can already carry, anything about reviewer precedence before knowing if domains collide, or anything about intent and audience that belongs in Goal's domain.
> 
> But the real principle is simpler — ask only where asking costs less than gating. A gate defers the question to runtime where the user has a real case in hand and can answer more easily. So the compile-time interview should only cover parameters where the answer shapes the artifact's structure itself, or where a runtime halt would be costly or late. I've been measuring materiality by consequence of getting it wrong, but that's missing the asking-cost dimension entirely. That's the core insight: add a deferability axis orthogonal to materiality.
> 
> Now looking at the auditable versus judgment-based distinction. The two-operator test is mostly auditable but still involves judgment calls like "plausible value" and "competent operators." The ambiguous tiering rule pushes everything to Tier 1, and since Tier 1 can never be defaulted, the system systematically inflates the question set. Under-classification is a spec failure, but over-classification is treated as nearly free — just one more question. Except that's wrong. It's one more question per parameter with no cap, and the cost model is fundamentally broken. The prompt explicitly assumes over-classification is cheap, which is exactly the assumption the Goal review just demolished.
> 
> The runtime quality is bounded to one cycle with no convergence-seeking loops, which is good discipline. But compile-time questioning is unbounded — Perfection applies its own best lesson to the downstream model while exempting itself. That's a strong inconsistency to flag.
> 
