> The real closing point is that fast is the actual product; slow is a holdover from before Goal existed. The /fast default is correct. The name itself is ironic — "Perfection" promises quality maximization, but §16.3 explicitly disclaims that capability, even mandating a notice that it can't detect quality failures. That contradiction is worth highlighting directly.

## First, a correction

My pre-read was wrong, and the way it was wrong is diagnostic.

I assumed from the name that Perfection was a refinement engine — something that takes an artifact and improves it. It isn't. Reading the prompt, Perfection is a **compiler**: it takes an under-specified goal and emits a fully specified downstream prompt, refusing to invent any value it wasn't given. There is no "improvement pass" anywhere in it. §6.2 is a single internal self-check; §6.3 isn't Perfection's quality loop at all, it's a *runtime* protocol Perfection writes into the child.

That mismatch is the first UX finding. The name mispredicted the artifact to a reader who was trying hard to predict it. And note the irony: the engine called Perfection ships a mandatory verbatim notice (§16.3) stating that it **cannot detect quality failures at all**. The name promises the one thing the prompt explicitly disclaims.

---

## The sentence

**"Perfection exists to compile a stated goal into an executable prompt in which every material unknown is either answered by the user or explicitly gated — and never invented."**

That is the whole engine. Tiers, ledgers, provenance mapping, verification checks, class routing — all of it is apparatus in service of one commitment: *no laundering of unsupplied facts*. That commitment is genuinely good, genuinely rare, and worth defending.

Everything below is about the gap between that sentence and the prompt.

---

## The deepest finding: two mechanisms for one problem, and the cheap one is subordinated

Perfection has **two** complete solutions to under-specification:

1. **Ask** (§3 slow track — pillar ledger, diagnostics, questions)
2. **Gate** (§4 — carry the unknown into the artifact as a blocking gate that halts at runtime)

Gating is a *complete* solution. §4 is airtight: a gate can never be resolved by model judgment, only by explicit runtime input. If every Tier-1 unknown is gated, correctness is preserved with **zero questions asked**. That's exactly what fast mode does — and fast mode is the default.

So asking is not a correctness requirement. It is a *convenience*: it front-loads answers so the artifact doesn't halt later.

Now look at how the prompt treats these two mechanisms:

- Gating: bounded, mechanical, one line of syntax.
- Asking: §3.1 **"NO TURN CAP"**, no cycle limit, no compiler-initiated fallback. §3.5 the compiler "may not initiate any exit, may not recommend one to end the session, and may not imply the session has run too long." §3.6 the sufficiency checkpoint "does not end the session and does not reduce the questions asked," and repeats every turn. §3.6 also forbids recommending compilation.

Read that as a user experience. The system reaches a state where it knows the specification is materially complete. It prints a heading saying **SPECIFICATION SUFFICIENT**. It is then *forbidden from suggesting you stop*, *forbidden from asking fewer questions*, and *required to reprint the same notice every turn*. It will do this indefinitely, because there is no cap.

This is interview creep constitutionalised. Not a leak — a mandate.

And it's an over-correction from a good instinct. Someone correctly worried about a compiler that bullies the user into a premature compile, and fixed it by banning the compiler from ever expressing an opinion about termination. The result is worse: a system that hides its own judgment about doneness from the person who needs it most.

**The prompt applies its own best lesson to the child and exempts itself.** §6.3: "EXIT: one cycle, then emit. No convergence-seeking loops." That is precisely the discipline slow mode lacks. Perfection knows that bounded iteration beats convergence-seeking. It writes that rule for the downstream model and refuses to live by it.

---

## The missing axis: materiality measures the wrong cost

§3.2 tiers parameters by **consequence of getting them wrong** (would two operators reach opposite conclusions?). That's a good test. But it's used to decide something it can't answer: **whether to ask**.

Deciding whether to ask requires a second, orthogonal question: **is this cheaper to answer now or later?**

Many Tier-1 parameters are *easier* to answer at runtime, with a real case in hand, than in the abstract at compile time. "What's the materiality threshold?" is a hard abstract question and an easy concrete one. Gating it isn't a degradation — for some parameters it's the better UX, because a halt with a named parameter is a *better prompt to the user* than a compile-time interview question.

So the tiering system is missing a dimension:

| | Cheap to answer now | Expensive/abstract now |
|---|---|---|
| **Shapes artifact structure** | **Ask** | Ask, but offer candidates |
| **Just supplies a value** | Ask if convenient | **Gate — don't ask** |

The bottom-right cell is where the interview should shrink. Nothing in §3 recognises it exists.

This is compounded by §3.2's explicit cost model, which is simply false:

> "Under-classification is a specification failure; over-classification costs only a question."

Over-classification costs a question **per parameter**, in a session with **no cap**, and Tier 1 **may never be defaulted**. Combined with the ambiguity rule (unclear → Tier 1), the system is engineered to inflate its own question set while telling itself the inflation is free. That single sentence is the engine of the creep. It is the exact assumption the Goal review demolished.

---

## Your eight questions

**1. What is Perfection fundamentally?**
A specification compiler with a no-invention guarantee. Its product is an artifact whose unknowns are *visible* rather than *filled in*. Its distinctive value is auditability: gates, the Assumptions Ledger, and provenance mapping let a user see exactly what wasn't supplied and what was substituted.

**2. What belongs here and nowhere else?**
- The ask-or-gate decision, and gate emission (§4).
- Provenance discipline — §9's no-laundering rule and §15.2's mapping. This is the crown jewel. "Format is not provenance" (17.5) is the single best line in the prompt.
- Tier-2 defaulting *with disclosure* — the Assumptions Ledger as a one-line-overturnable artifact.
- Output-class routing and the machinery-does-not-ship rule (§16.1). The exhaustive enumeration of pure-compile-time sections is excellent and rare.
- Compile-time verification with stated failure conditions (§10's framing rule, "a check with no failure condition is not a check," is exemplary).

**3. What should be removed?**

- **§3.1 NO TURN CAP.** Replace with a hard bound. Not a soft norm — a number. Given gates exist, three to five diagnostic turns is defensible; everything unresolved at the bound gets gated. The correctness guarantee is untouched because §4 does the work.
- **§3.5's gag on the compiler.** Delete "may not recommend one to end the session." Replace with a rule that the compiler must *state its assessment once* when sufficiency is reached, then not repeat it.
- **§3.6's repetition.** The checkpoint should fire **once**, and the turn it fires should *reduce* questions to the remaining refinement items only. As written it is a notice that changes nothing, printed forever.
- **§7.6 SCOPE OF SUBSTITUTION + CONDITIONAL SHIPPING.** This is a conditional-shipping rule governing a scope limitation on a substitution rule for a sub-clause of a Tier-2 source parameter. Three levels of nesting on a case that will approximately never fire. Cut to the default line: "tier rank is not overcome by volume of agreement."
- **§6.3 RESIDUAL PRECEDENCE as blocking Tier 1.** A user who sensibly says "legal owns obligations, comms owns tone" is then blocked until they answer a set-theory question about disagreements falling outside all domains *or* inside two simultaneously. Fix with the prompt's own tool: §4's ON_MISSING SCOPE. Gate it, scoped — "halt only if two reviewers disagree outside or across named domains." Correctness preserved, question eliminated, artifact still compiles.
- **§10 CROSS-REFERENCE.** This checks that §n references inside *the compiler's own prose* resolve. That's authoring-time hygiene for whoever maintains Perfection, not a property of the compiled artifact. It shouldn't consume a runtime verification slot.
- **§16.3's verbatim mandatory notice, in every compile.** Six lines of unchanging boilerplate at the top of every artifact. The intent is honesty; the effect is that users learn to skip the Compile Header — which is where the Assumptions Ledger and gates live. Mandatory invariant text degrades attention to adjacent variable text. Compress to one line plus the recompile triggers, or ship it only where a rubric is absent (where the consequence is actually total, per its own admission).

**4. What should Perfection ask?**
Only parameters that meet **both** tests: (a) material, and (b) structure-shaping or genuinely cheaper now than at runtime. Concretely: adjudicative rubric dimensions, reviewer refusals and authority, conflict precedence between sources, retrieval-failure behaviour. These change the artifact's *shape*, not just its values.

And §3.3's candidate-offering rule is the right form — 2-4 concrete options drawn from the user's own material, answerable in one line. That should be the *dominant* question form, not the fallback for failed open questions.

**5. What should it never ask?**
- Anything Goal owns: intent, audience, purpose, success criteria, scope. P1 Role/Bounds and P2 Logic are the leakage points.
- Tier 3 — already correctly banned.
- Anything a scoped gate can carry at equal or lower user cost. This is the new prohibition and it's the one that shrinks the interview.
- Edge-case-of-an-edge-case questions (residual precedence, concurrence substitution) before the base case is even established.
- Anything after the sufficiency threshold, unless the user asks to continue.

**6. Does the current design encourage interview creep?**
It mandates it. Beyond §3.1/§3.5/§3.6 above, note that **§3.3's progress metric is self-defeating**. It requires each turn to "strictly reduce the unresolved set," then permits satisfying that by "decompos[ing] one unresolved item into narrower sub-questions." Decomposition *increases* the item count. The rule licenses growth as proof of progress. A user can answer honestly every turn and watch the open-item list get longer while the system reports discipline.

**7. Is it over-engineered?**
The core is not. The periphery is, in a specific and diagnosable way: the deepest nesting sits on the **rarest** cases. §7.6's three-layer substitution scoping, §6.3's residual precedence, §7's dual-scoped applicability branches — these are the most intricate rules in the document and govern the least frequent situations. Meanwhile the most common invocation (17.6: `/fast`, draft a guide) is the case that needed a dedicated counter-example to stop the machinery compiling to a no-op. That's the tell. When your simplest case requires a worked example proving it doesn't break, complexity has outrun its base case.

Also, §17.6 as written tags the *entire* deliverable: rubric absence means no rubric governs any of it, so "mark affected output per §4 coverage-gap routing" marks everything. The user gets a training guide littered with `<coverage_gap ref="G1"/>`. One disclosure row in the ledger conveys the same information without vandalising the deliverable.

**8. Is it optimising things that don't matter?**
Apply the test — *would the user notice if this were skipped?*

| Feature | Noticed if removed? |
|---|---|
| Blocking gates on real thresholds | **Yes** — prevents invented determinations |
| Assumptions Ledger | **Yes** — one-line overturnable, high value |
| Provenance mapping (§15.2) | **Yes** — prevents laundering |
| Machinery-does-not-ship (§16.1) | **Yes** — child artifacts stay clean |
| Adaptive fencing (§12) | No, but nearly free — keep |
| Verbatim drift notice, every compile | **No** |
| Per-turn pillar ledger with per-pillar reasons | **No** — ~20 lines of apparatus per 1-3 questions |
| Repeating sufficiency checkpoint | **No** — actively harmful |
| §7.6 substitution scope + conditional shipping | **No** |
| §10 CROSS-REFERENCE | **No** — wrong audience |

The pattern: Perfection optimises *disclosure surface* — completeness of stated caveats — at the cost of *attention*. Attention is the scarce resource. Every mandatory invariant paragraph spends it, and the things worth reading (gates, ledger rows) are what go unread.

---

## The architectural question you should decide before anything else

Goal is closed and owns discovery. Perfection's slow track is an interview. So: **why does slow mode still exist?**

Two coherent answers, and you must pick:

**(a) Slow mode dies.** Goal interviews, bounded. Perfection compiles what it receives and gates the rest. This is the clean post-Goal architecture, and **fast mode already is it** — compile on Turn 1, gate everything material, no conversational output. Fast mode is Perfection expressing its identity correctly. Slow mode is a pre-Goal artifact, retained past the point where the pipeline made it necessary.

**(b) Slow mode survives, narrowly and bounded** — restricted to parameters Goal structurally cannot produce, because they're properties of the compiled artifact rather than the user's intent: reviewer authority, rubric failure criteria, source conflict precedence. Hard turn cap. Gate on exhaustion.

Either way, one integration issue needs resolving. §15.1 says an unprovenanced upstream payload is "treated as fully unresolved: every determinative field... routed to questions (slow) or gates (fast)." If Goal is the upstream and doesn't emit per-field provenance, then **Goal interviews the user, and Perfection re-interviews the user about Goal's answers.** The pipeline double-interviews by design, and §15.1's FAST-PATH PENALTY says so out loud: "it does not reduce work here, it relocates it."

§15.2 already has the fix — "stated by user → supplied; adopt." The requirement is on Goal's output contract: emit per-field provenance. That's a one-line integration requirement that removes an entire redundant interview from the user's experience. If Goal is truly closed and can't emit it, that's the highest-value thing to reopen it for.

---

## What I'd keep untouched

The no-invention core, the two-operator test as a *tiering* heuristic, gates and gate routing, the Assumptions Ledger's one-line-overturnable design, §15.2's provenance mapping and §15.3's mechanism test ("adjectival edges are not constraints" is worth more than most of §7), §16.1's exhaustive shipping enumeration, §10's "a check with no failure condition is not a check," `/fast` as default, and the closed error-code registry.

That's a strong engine. It's carrying an unbounded interview it doesn't need, a name that mispredicts it, and roughly 25% ceremony concentrated on its rarest cases.

## On the name

`Perfection` promises quality maximisation. The engine delivers *specification completeness* — and §16.3 explicitly admits it cannot detect quality failures. The name also recruits the maximalist behaviour visible in §3.1 and §7.6, because prompts live up to their titles. **Compile**, **Spec**, or **Contract** would each describe the artifact and argue *for* termination rather than against it.
